            <ArticleTitle>[Complete third nerve paralysis as clinical presentation of multiple sclerosis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>159-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An acute attack of cephalea and third nerve palsy with pupillary involvement may be caused by a variety of entities, but aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery must certainly be ruled out. We describe a 22 year old patient in whom this clinical profile was an unusual first sign of multiple sclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
